Basic Concepts and Principles of Sustainable Development Design
In the definition of sustainable development adopted by the world community and approved by the United Nations (UN), two concepts are fixed (Bolshakov et al., 2019): an opportunity and a need necessary for the preservation and development of systems of any nature and purpose. The pair ìopportunity ñ needî denotes a contradiction, the resolution of which is carried out by minimizing the difference between needs and opportunities with the help of more advanced ideas, projects and technologies. In the next cycle, the process is repeated with other increased characteristics of opportunities ñ needs. Considering the relationship between needs and opportunities, we begin by answering simple questions of sustainable development design: What is the initial state of the system? What is the goal of sustainable development? Why is the transition to sustainable development necessary? What problems exist? How is sustainable development defined? Who, where and when implements sustainable development? How sustainable development is measured? In general, the main stages of designing sustainable development are as follows: ñ Definition and calculation of the existing state or definition of opportunities ñ ìModel parameters for the initial timeî; ñ Definition and calculation of the required state or definition of needs ñ ìParameters of the model in accordance with the development criteriaî; ñ Definition, calculation and formulation of problems; ñ Formation of a strategy and forecast of critical situations; ñ Defining parameters and methods from calculation; ñ Formation of the management system ñ decision planning, parameter measurement, measurement control. The modern real world is a system of nested cycles, accompanied by crises and conflicts of various scales, tearing apart the chrono-holistic process of development into separate ëpiecesí of rise and fall. These ëpiecesí are difficult to link to sustainable development. Moreover, it is impossible to give an example of the sustainable development of any living socio-economic object throughout the entire time of its life. And yet, the life


