of the Earth as a continuous process over 4 billion years demonstrates an amazing ability to maintain continuous development, despite a huge number of factors of negative, and sometimes catastrophic external and internal impact. This ability to resolve contradiction is the fundamental law of the cyclical conservation of the development of living systems (Bauer, 2002). The consequences of this law and its projections are many other laws ñ the law of the cyclical change of biological species, the law of the cyclical change of generations, the law of the cyclical change of technologies, the law of cyclical economic development, and others. All of them make it possible to manage the process of transition from one quality to another, the process of overcoming the crisis in order to maintain the further development of a living system in the long term. Under the conditions of negative influences, the development of life is preserved at each cycle of its existence, if there is an integral non-decreasing increase in the efficiency of using the consumed power (resources). Development is sustainable in a certain cycle of the existence of a living system, if during this period there is a non-decreasing increase in the efficiency of using the consumed power (Shamaeva, 2019). In the transition between cycles, the chronointegrity of development is destroyed, dimensional (spatio-temporal) gaps arise ñ crises that require breakthrough management technologies, when sustainable development becomes a strategic goal of managing the way out of the crisis. Without management and outside of management, it is fundamentally impossible to move into a new quality and ensure sustainable development in the long term, covering the system of cycles ñ crises of modern world civilization (Karibaev et al., 2019). Any sciencebased strategy must use scientific knowledge based on universal laws. Comprehension and correct application of these laws in practice will allow effectively implementing the strategy. Today, there is a fear that the current idea of sustainable development may lead to the repetition of strategic mistakes in choosing the development trajectory of individual countries and the world community as a whole.
Experience of Designing Sustainable Development in the World
The first attempt to move the world community to an integrated approach to considering the interaction of society with the environment was the international conference on environment and development, convened by the UN (1972). Participants recognized the existence of a relationship between the need to create a safe environment for humans and economic development (UN, 1972). In 1987, at the 42nd session of the UN General Assembly, the basic principle of sustainable development was adopted (UN, 1987). Sustainable development was defined in the Brundtland Report as development that meets the needs of the present generation without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs (Brundtland, 1987). In 1992, the UN convened the second international conference on environment and development in Rio de Janeiro, where it adopted a number of fundamental documents outlining the ideology of sustainable development (UN, 1992). The Club of Romeís report ìThe Limits to Growthî (Meadows et al., 1972) was the first to model our planetís interconnected systems and made clear tendencies in population, industrialization, resource use and pollution. ìThe Limits to Growthî contains the follow main messages: First, the environmental impact of human society had become heavier between 1900 and 1972 due to both an increase in the number of humans


