and the amount of resources consumed and pollution. Second, our planet is physically limited, and that humanity cannot continue to use more physical resources and generate more emissions than nature is capable of supplying in a sustainable manner. Third, the human ecological footprint will soon overshoot the carrying capacity of the planet. Fourth, we will have to move back into a sustainable state, either through ëmanaged declineí of activity, or through ëcollapseí of nature or the market. Fifth, humanity must act now and today. As a result of the work of the Club of Rome, an interpretation of sustainable development has arisen, which is often called the ëtriune concept of sustainable developmentí, highlighting the interdependence of the three pillars of sustainable development: environmental, social and economic. Sustainable development becomes achievable only when the stability of each of the three listed components is formed. Later on, the concept of economic development without growth or zero economic growth has become widespread. In addition, the supporters of this concept believe that economic growth leads to disruption of the biosphere of human life and is limited due to the planetís lack of raw materials and fuel resources (Pestel, 1989). The conclusions of the report are based on the idea that the mission of human civilization on the Earth is not positive, it is destructive, and therefore it is necessary to limit its birth rate and reduce human activity. The second important point of the report is that a single picture of the world is considered at the level of three separate systems (Economics, Ecology and Society) and their separate reference cannot give an idea and understanding of the performance results and development perspectives of the whole socio-economic system. This is not in line with a systematic approach. The report has become the basis for further development of the concept of sustainable development. The UN has endorsed the 2030 Agenda, which is a call to action to protect the planet, end poverty and guarantee human well-being. According to the United Nations Communication Group (UNCG), the SDG is a world call to action to end poverty, protect the planet in peace and prosperity by 2030. In 2016, SDGs were adopted by 193 countries to promote economic growth, to ensure social inclusion and environmental protection. Club of Rome leaders Ernst von Weizs‰cker and Anders Wijkman in 2018 presented a joint report titled ìCome On!î (Weizs‰cker & Wijkman, 2017). The report deals with the decline of capitalist civilization in the form in which it exists at the moment ñ with its own guidelines for expanding production and making a profit. It has been criticized that GDP reflects spending rather than well-being or subjective happiness, and does not see the benefits that exist outside the market. It has been determined that the only thing that measures GDP is the speed at which money moves in the economy. A breakthrough in understanding the sustainable development of the world was the ideas that served as the starting point for drawing up the report: ó the concept of ëfull worldí proposed by the American ecologist and economist Herman Daly (Daly, 2015). Human civilization was formed in the conditions of an ëempty worldí ñ a world of unexplored territories and an excess of resources; ó concepts of living socio-economic systems proposed by F. Capra (Capra & Jakobsen, 2017). According to H. Daly (2015), the economy is an open subsystem of a larger ecosphere, which is finite, non-growing, and materially closed, although open to a continual, but non-growing, throughput of solar energy. When the economy grows in physical


