The Need for a Change in Thinking and Attitude Based on Education
According to the authors of the article, there is a clear need for changes in the consciousness and actions of each individual, as well as in the society as a whole, in relation to the concept of sustainability, both theoretically and practically. In Europe, three revolutions are simultaneously under way which are bound to alter the strategic challenges that Europe will have to address: (1) an economic and technological revolution: the convergence of digital, biological and industrial technologies and the digital tools; (2) a social and democratic revolution: more empowered and better connected individuals will be more creative, more dynamic, less wedded to life-time jobs, but also more demanding and critical; (3) a geopolitical revolution: Asiaís rise will continue and the roughly two centuries of global dominance by the European continent and the United States are drawing to a close (ESPAS, 2015). Several authors write about the new competences and skills in the 21st century, which include the digital, social, basic and contextual skills, including scientific literacy. Scientific literacy as an element of employability skills is growing nowadays, as science has become part of peopleís everyday life and it is expected that the importance of scientific literacy will only increase in the future (Bikse et al., 2022). The environmental problems are becoming part of a wider problem of peopleís attitudes towards the environment. They are socially conditioned and their causes are found in the approaches that are based on external factors rather than internal, i.e., they are found in environmental factors such as demography and geography, economy, politics, education, and culture (Andi„ & Vorkapi„, 2017). Director-General of UNESCO A. Azoul in the ìReport of the International Commission on the Future of Educationî emphasizes that we must act urgently to change the current course, because the future of people depends on the future of the planet, and both are at risk. A new social contract for education is being proposed, which aims to renew our relationship with each other, with the planet and with technology. This new social contract is our opportunity to take the responsibility for the injustices of the past and transform the future. Above all, it is based on the right to quality education throughout life, embracing teaching and learning as a shared societal endeavor and thus a common good (UNESCO, 2021).


